<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karl I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Charles I of Austria · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg-lorraine">House of Habsburg-Lorraine</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karl I, or Charles I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, was the last Emperor of Austria and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, reigning from 1916 to 1918. He sought a separate peace during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> without success. With defeat in 1918 he withdrew from state affairs as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> dissolved.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-i.jpg" alt="Karl I" width="293" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hermann Clemens Kosel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_I.,_Kaiser_von_%C3%96sterreich_(1887-1922).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles was born on 17 August 1887 at the Castle of Persenbeug in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>, the son of Archduke Otto Franz and Princess Maria Josepha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. He was raised a devout Catholic and, unusually for the imperial family, attended a public <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a>, the Schottengymnasium, before entering the army. From 1906 to 1908 he served as an officer chiefly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, where he studied law and political science. On 21 October 1911 he married Princess Zita of Bourbon-Parma. He became heir presumptive in 1914 when his uncle Archduke Franz Ferdinand was assassinated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a>, an event that precipitated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Charles succeeded his grand-uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a> on 21 November 1916, took the title of Supreme Commander of the army on 2 December and was crowned King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> on 30 December. In 1917 he secretly began peace negotiations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> through his brother-in-law Prince Sixtus of Bourbon-Parma, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> insisted that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> recognise Italian territorial claims and he refused. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixtus-affair">Sixtus Affair</a> leaked in April 1918, Charles denied involvement until French Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-clemenceau">Georges Clemenceau</a> published letters signed by him. Foreign minister Count Czernin resigned, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> had to give <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> full control of its armed forces, factories and railways.</p><p>From the start Charles favoured a third, Croatian, political entity beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, but the Hungarian Parliament vetoed the proposals. On 14 October 1918 he issued a People's Manifesto, and on 29 October the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-parliament">Croatian Sabor</a> ended all ties with Hungary and Austria. On 11 November 1918 he issued a proclamation recognising the Austrian people's right to determine the form of the state and relinquishing every participation in the administration, although the word abdication was never used. He wrote privately that he had not abdicated and never would. In April 1919 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> dethroned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and banished him from German-Austria for life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Charles went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and in 1921 made two attempts to reclaim the Hungarian throne, which failed largely because the regent, Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miklos-horthy">Miklós Horthy</a>, refused his support. He was arrested and, on 19 November 1921, arrived at his last place of exile, the Portuguese island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madeira">Madeira</a>, where he died of respiratory failure on 1 April 1922. Historians have been mixed in their judgements. Helmut Rumpler called him far too weak for the challenges facing him, while others see a brave and honourable man who tried to stop the war. The Catholic Church beatified him in 2004.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles I of Austria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His young successor <b>Karl I</b> sought a negotiated peace through the secret Sixtus Affair of 1917 and in October 1918 offered to turn Austria into a federation of nationalities, but found neither Germany nor the Allies receptive.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 12 November 1918, the day after <b>Emperor Charles I</b> withdrew from government, it proclaimed the Republic of German-Austria, declaring it part of the German Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-german-austria">Republic of German-Austria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Persenbeug Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in Funchal, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.19,15.08&amp;year=1917" aria-label="Persenbeug Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4900 478 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Karl I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5350 748h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5350 748h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.19,15.08&amp;year=1917">What was here in 1917? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
